    curated_articles += [
        {"title": "Stop the Four Horsemen: Argue Without Damaging Your Marriage", "tag": "Relationships",
         "img": None, "emoji": "\U0001F495", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg, #FF416C, #8E2DE2)",
         "excerpt": "Criticism, contempt, defensiveness and stonewalling wear a relationship down \u2014 and each one has a simple, learnable antidote.",
         "full": "Decades of research on couples by Drs. John and Julie Gottman found four communication habits that most reliably predict a relationship in trouble: <b>criticism</b> (attacking your partner's character instead of raising one specific issue), <b>contempt</b> (talking down to your partner from a position of superiority), <b>defensiveness</b> (deflecting blame and playing the victim) and <b>stonewalling</b> (shutting down and going silent).<br><br>The good news is that each has an antidote. Replace criticism with a <b>gentle start-up</b> \u2014 \"I felt worried when\u2026\" instead of \"You always\u2026\". Replace contempt with a daily <b>culture of appreciation</b>. Replace defensiveness by <b>taking responsibility</b> for even a small part of the problem. And replace stonewalling with a <b>self-soothing break</b> \u2014 agree to pause for 20\u201330 minutes, then come back to the conversation.<br><br>Spotting the pattern early, and repairing quickly afterwards, matters far more than never arguing at all."},
        {"title": "Money After Marriage: Joint, Separate or Both?", "tag": "Money & Finance",
         "img": None, "emoji": "\U0001F4B0", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg, #0BA360, #2193B0)",
         "excerpt": "How couples combine (or don't combine) bank accounts, and the spending-limit trick that avoids everyday arguments.",
         "full": "There is no single right way to manage money as a couple. Some couples merge everything into joint accounts for full transparency; others keep separate accounts for independence; many choose a hybrid \u2014 one joint account for rent, bills and shared goals, plus personal accounts for individual spending.<br><br>Whatever you choose, agree on it openly <i>before</i> merging. Remember that with a joint account both people are usually responsible for the balance and any debt on it. Many couples also set a <b>spending limit</b> \u2014 any purchase above an agreed amount is discussed first \u2014 so nobody has to justify every coffee, but big decisions are still shared.<br><br>Finally, revisit the arrangement every year or after big changes such as a new job, a home purchase or a baby."},
    ]

    # ---- The best articles from around the world for each topic. "Read Full
    # Article" opens the ORIGINAL publisher's page in a new tab, so people get
